1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,521
You plug a USB gamepad into your Android phone.

2
00:00:03,521 --> 00:00:04,687
The buttons work.

3
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:05,624
But the rumble?

4
00:00:05,624 --> 00:00:06,333
Nothing.

5
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:09,098
If that sounds familiar, this video is for you.

6
00:00:09,098 --> 00:00:15,480
Meet NeuroPad: a free, open source app that brings rumble back to USB gamepads on Android.

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:16,778
And you don't need root.

8
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:19,346
Here's the problem.

9
00:00:19,346 --> 00:00:28,769
When you connect a USB-C gamepad to an Android phone, Android passes the buttons through just fine, but it doesn't pass rumble on to most USB controllers.

10
00:00:28,769 --> 00:00:33,228
So the game asks your controller to vibrate, and the motors never get the message.

11
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:37,303
NeuroPad fixes this with a simple idea.

12
00:00:37,303 --> 00:00:43,505
It shows your games a virtual wireless controller, an Xbox or PlayStation pad that Android fully supports.

13
00:00:43,505 --> 00:00:46,894
Your real USB gamepad drives that virtual controller.

14
00:00:46,894 --> 00:00:53,685
And every rumble the game produces is sent straight back to the motors in your real pad, within a few milliseconds.

15
00:00:55,283 --> 00:00:58,539
And the best part: this is confirmed on real hardware.

16
00:00:58,539 --> 00:01:04,692
NeuroPad was tested on a Nothing Phone 2 running Android 16, with a GameSir G8 Plus.

17
00:01:04,692 --> 00:01:08,046
Input, the virtual controller, and rumble all work.

18
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:11,123
Users report the Razer Kishi Ultra working too.

19
00:01:11,123 --> 00:01:12,553
The problem is fixed.

20
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:15,518
Let's take a look at the app.

21
00:01:15,518 --> 00:01:19,028
The Home screen has one big power button that starts the bridge.

22
00:01:19,028 --> 00:01:24,845
A checklist shows system access, your USB gamepad, and the virtual controller at a glance.

23
00:01:24,845 --> 00:01:30,963
You choose which controller games should see, and live meters show the heavy and light motors as they rumble.

24
00:01:30,963 --> 00:01:33,956
You can even test each motor without opening a game.

25
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:43,779
There are five controller profiles: Xbox 360, Xbox One, Xbox Series, DualShock 4, and DualSense.

26
00:01:43,779 --> 00:01:45,846
Games show the matching button prompts.

27
00:01:45,846 --> 00:01:49,944
If one game doesn't rumble with one profile, simply try another.

28
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:57,690
The Controller tab shows your input live: sticks, triggers and buttons, exactly as they arrive.

29
00:01:57,690 --> 00:02:02,738
You can switch to a Nintendo layout or build a fully custom one, including back paddles.

30
00:02:02,738 --> 00:02:07,966
And the bridge statistics show how fast it is: about a fifth of a millisecond per packet.

31
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:15,666
To create a virtual controller, NeuroPad needs the rights of Android's shell user, but not root.

32
00:02:15,666 --> 00:02:23,142
On Android 11 or newer, pair once with Wireless debugging and the app sets everything up by itself, no computer required.

33
00:02:23,142 --> 00:02:27,036
On any Android version, the one-click PC installer does the job.

34
00:02:27,036 --> 00:02:28,959
And rooted phones are supported too.

35
00:02:28,959 --> 00:02:35,642
Nothing ever leaves your phone: the app only talks to itself, on 127.0.0.1.

36
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:43,618
In Settings, you can tune the rumble strength, and stretch very short pulses so small effects are actually felt.

37
00:02:43,618 --> 00:02:48,041
Ignore system vibration keeps keyboard taps from shaking your controller.

38
00:02:48,041 --> 00:02:53,426
And for games that only vibrate the phone, NeuroPad can mirror that vibration to your gamepad.

39
00:02:55,017 --> 00:03:00,796
Version 2 is a complete open source rewrite, and it fixes the problems people ran into before.

40
00:03:00,796 --> 00:03:04,041
Long rumbles are no longer cut off after half a second.

41
00:03:04,041 --> 00:03:07,286
Rumble no longer goes missing after a notification.

42
00:03:07,286 --> 00:03:11,000
Launcher taps and keyboard clicks no longer shake the gamepad.

43
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,219
Short effects are no longer weak, and long rumbles no longer stutter.

44
00:03:16,817 --> 00:03:22,644
NeuroPad works with XInput, or Xbox mode, USB gamepads that have rumble motors.

45
00:03:22,644 --> 00:03:29,062
That covers many controllers from GameSir, Razer, 8BitDo, Flydigi, and Microsoft.

46
00:03:29,062 --> 00:03:33,281
If your controller has a mode switch, set it to Xbox or XInput.

47
00:03:34,883 --> 00:03:42,622
To get NeuroPad, open the GitHub repository, NeuroPad Virtual GamePad Simulation, and download the latest release.

48
00:03:42,622 --> 00:03:49,545
Use the one-click installer if you have a computer nearby, or install the APK straight on the phone on Android 11 and up.

49
00:03:49,545 --> 00:03:53,499
Then plug in your gamepad, open NeuroPad, and tap the power button.

50
00:03:53,499 --> 00:03:54,220
That's it.

51
00:03:55,817 --> 00:03:59,589
NeuroPad is free, has no ads, and needs no account.

52
00:03:59,589 --> 00:04:07,606
If it brought rumble back to your controller, a star on GitHub helps other people find it, and you can support development on Ko-fi or PayPal.

53
00:04:07,606 --> 00:04:09,240
Tried it with another controller?

54
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:10,598
Tell us in the comments.

55
00:04:10,598 --> 00:04:11,704
Thanks for watching!
